%N A073230 Decimal expansion of (1/e)^e.
%C A073230 (1/e)^e = e^(-e) = 1/(e^e) (reciprocal of A073226).
%C A073230 The power tower function f(x)=x^(x^(x^...)) is defined on the closed interval [e^(-e),e^(1/e)]. - _Lekraj Beedassy_, Mar 17 2005
%D A073230 Paul Halmos, "Problems for Mathematicians, Young and Old", Dolciani Mathematical Expositions, 1991, Solution to problem 8A (Power Tower), p. 240.
%D A073230 François Le Lionnais, Les Nombres Remarquables, Paris, Hermann, 1983, p. 22.
%H A073230 G. C. Greubel, <a href="/A073230/b073230.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A073230 R. Arthur Knoebel, <a href="http://www.jstor.org/stable/2320546">Exponentials Reiterated</a>, The American Mathematical Monthly, Vol. 88, No. 4 (1981), pp. 235-252.
%H A073230 Fan Ludwig, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/309785.309801">On continuing powers, superexponents and an exponential iterator></a>, ACM SIGAPL APL Quote Quad, Vol. 28, No. 2 (1997), pp. 23-27.
%H A073230 Jonathan Sondow and Diego Marques, <a href="https://publikacio.uni-eszterhazy.hu/3191/">Algebraic and transcendental solutions of some exponential equations</a>, Annales Mathematicae et Informaticae 37 (2010) 151-164; see the Appendix.
%H A073230 Roger Voles, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/3619042">An Exploration of Hyperpower Equations ^{n}x=^{n}y</a>, The Mathematical Gazette, Vol. 83, No. 497 (1999), pp. 210-215.
